    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d367">

                <pb facs="0562" n="486" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg486"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.015311/5</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Luther</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-08-12">August 12, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: Having reference to your
                    call at the Department on June twenty-eighth in connection with an article <pb
                        facs="0563" n="487" xml:id="pg487"/> which appeared in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York American</hi> on May thirty-first stating that
                    the authorities of Newark, New Jersey, prohibited the display of the German
                    swastika at a gathering of American-German singing societies at that place on
                    Decoration Day, I now desire to inform you that the matter was at once taken up
                    with the Governor of New Jersey, who in a letter to the Department dated July
                        twenty-seventh<anchor n="46" xml:id="d367-f1"/> encloses a report on the
                    incident made to him by Mr. Michael P. Duffy, Director of Public Safety of
                    Newark.</p>
                <p>A copy of Mr. Duffy’s report is transmitted herewith<anchor n="46"
                        xml:id="d367-f2"/> from which it would appear that the determination to omit
                    the swastika in the display of flags was not on the part of the officials of the
                    City of Newark but was taken by those in charge of the celebration.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cordell Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
